# Web security (Django docs + OWASP Cheat Sheet)

Sources: [Django security](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/topics/security/), [deployment checklist](https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/stable/howto/deployment/checklist/), [OWASP Django Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Django_Security_Cheat_Sheet.html).

This repo is **FastAPI**, but the same threats apply. Map Django settings to reverse-proxy / Starlette middleware when relevant.

## Never trust user input

- Validate and sanitize all external input (body, query, headers, files, env)
- Prefer allowlists and schema validation (Pydantic) over ad-hoc checks

## XSS

- Escape HTML when rendering user content; never mark untrusted data as safe
- Avoid injecting raw user strings into HTML/JS contexts
- Be careful with Markdown/HTML from LLMs if ever rendered in a browser

## CSRF

- Protect cookie-based session state-changing requests (CSRF tokens / SameSite)
- For pure Bearer/API-key JSON APIs without cookies, CSRF risk is lower — do not add fake CSRF theater; do not introduce cookie auth without CSRF

## SQL injection

- Use parameterized queries / ORM only; never f-string or concatenate SQL
- Avoid raw SQL unless parameters are bound; never interpolate user input

## Clickjacking / headers

- Prefer `X-Frame-Options: DENY` (or CSP `frame-ancestors`) unless embedding is required
- Enable `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, HSTS when serving HTTPS

## HTTPS / cookies / Host

- Production behind HTTPS; redirect HTTP→HTTPS at the edge when possible
- Secure cookies: `Secure`, `HttpOnly`, `SameSite` when using sessions
- Validate Host / configure trusted hosts; never trust raw `Host` for security decisions
- Be extremely careful with forwarded proto/host headers from proxies

## Secrets & debug

- Never ship `DEBUG=True` (or verbose exception bodies) in production
- Secrets (`SECRET_KEY`, API keys) only via env / secret manager — never in git
- Rotate keys if exposed; fail closed when required secrets are missing

## Uploads & auth

- Limit upload size/type; serve user content from a separate domain when possible
- Rate-limit auth and expensive endpoints (e.g. LLM calls)
- Authorize at the data layer (broken access control is app logic, not framework magic)

## Dependencies

- Keep framework and deps patched; audit lockfiles in CI when practical
